  <!-- SECTION 1: What custom drapery is, definitionally -->
  <article class="bg-white py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="container-narrow prose-cfc">
      <h2 id="what-is-custom-drapery">What custom drapery is, and what it isn't</h2>
      <p>Custom drapery is fabric window-covering built to your exact opening — your window's exact width, your ceiling height, your floor's exact break point, your hardware's exact projection. It is not a panel you pull off a shelf and clip to a rod. Three categories of drapery exist on the market, and they are easy to confuse:</p>

      <h3>Ready-made drapery</h3>
      <p>Pre-sewn panels sold in standard widths (typically 50 inches) and four standard lengths (63, 84, 95, 108 inches). Available at Pottery Barn, West Elm, Target, Amazon. Cost: $30–$300 per panel. If your window matches the standard, the panel works. If it does not — and most St. Pete windows don't — the panel gaps, drags, or floats. There is no fix; it is a manufactured product, not a tailored one.</p>

      <h3>Semi-custom drapery</h3>
      <p>Sold by Pottery Barn, Restoration Hardware, Calico Corners, and similar. You pick from a limited fabric list and choose from preset widths and lengths. Slightly closer to your actual window, but still constrained to the supplier's options. Cost: $200–$700 per panel. The fabric library is narrower than a true custom workroom and the install is not included.</p>

      <h3>Fully custom drapery (what we make)</h3>
      <p>Sewn to your specific measurements in your fabric of choice. The fabric library covers thousands of patterns from the major textile houses — Kravet, Stout, Lee Jofa, Brunschwig &amp; Fils, GP &amp; J Baker, plus performance fabrics like Crypton. Lining, interlining, header style, hem treatment, and hardware are all specified per project. Measured by a designer, sewn in a workroom, installed by the same team. Cost: $400–$1,500+ per window installed. This is what people in St. Petersburg search for when they search "<strong>custom drapery St. Petersburg</strong>."</p>

      <h2 id="why-st-pete">Why custom drapery matters more in St. Petersburg than in most cities</h2>
      <p>Most national drapery advice was written for temperate climates. Florida is not temperate. Four factors push St. Pete homeowners toward custom-made:</p>

      <h3>The Florida sun degrades cheap fabric</h3>
      <p>St. Petersburg averages 361 sunny days a year and gets some of the highest UV indices in the continental United States. Ready-made drapery is typically unlined or single-lined with unrated linings. Within 18–36 months, the leading edge of the panel — the part that faces the window glass — fades, weakens, and starts to crack. Custom drapery uses UV-rated linings (Hanes Pearl, Roc-Lon Sundown) and treated fabrics that are tested for sustained UV exposure. The cost difference at the start is meaningful; the cost difference over ten years favors custom heavily.</p>

      <h3>Coastal salt air corrodes hardware and rots fabric</h3>
      <p>Homes in Snell Isle, Tierra Verde, Shore Acres, Sand Key, St. Pete Beach, Treasure Island, and Belleair Shore deal with salt-air exposure year-round. Cheap drapery hardware corrodes within five years; cheap fabric mildews. Our coastal projects use marine-grade hardware, moisture-resistant interlinings, and synthetic-blend or treated natural fabrics rated for salt environments.</p>

      <h3>St. Pete's architectural mix demands different solutions</h3>
      <p>A 1920s Historic Old Northeast Craftsman has 7-foot ceilings, vintage trim, and divided-light windows. A Downtown St. Pete high-rise condo has 10-foot ceilings, floor-to-ceiling glass, and no trim. A Snell Isle Mediterranean Revival has arched transoms, deep window returns, and stucco walls. Off-the-rack drapery cannot serve all three. Custom can.</p>

      <h3>Hurricane season changes how drapery should be designed</h3>
      <p>From June through November, every St. Pete homeowner should know how their drapery comes down. We design hardware systems for coastal Pinellas homes that disconnect from the bracket in under five minutes — panels go in a closet, hardware stays. Our installers walk every coastal-zone client through the removal at install day. Off-the-rack drapery on traditional rod sets requires unscrewing every bracket. The difference matters on a Wednesday afternoon when the storm cone shifts.</p>

      <h2 id="types-of-drapery">The types of custom drapery we make in St. Petersburg</h2>
      <p>Every St. Pete room has different needs. The breakdown of what we make and who it's for:</p>

      <h3>Sheer drapery panels</h3>
      <p>Lightweight, translucent panels that filter Florida sun without blocking the view. Mounted alone or layered behind a heavier drapery layer. Best for rooms that get west or south sun, where you want softness but not full privacy. Common fabrics: Kravet linen sheers, Stout textured weaves, Belgian linen blends.</p>

      <h3>Blackout drapery</h3>
      <p>Lined with a blackout interlining that blocks 99%+ of visible light. Built so the leading edge of the panel seals against the wall (not the case with most off-the-rack blackout panels). Critical for bedrooms, nurseries, home theaters, and any shift-work household. We pair blackout drapery with a daytime sheer in many bedrooms so the room can be soft in the morning and dark at night.</p>

      <h3>Designer drapery</h3>
      <p>Rich, formal panels in silks, embroidered patterns, velvets, or decorative jacquards. This is where the room comes together for clients who want their drapery to read as a design feature, not background. Fabric brands: Brunschwig &amp; Fils, Lee Jofa, GP &amp; J Baker, Kravet Couture. Often paired with custom valances, cornices, or contrast trim.</p>

      <h3>Motorized drapery</h3>
      <p>Operated by wall switch, remote, smartphone app, or smart-home integration (Lutron, Apple HomeKit, Google Home, Alexa). Best for windows over 8 feet, sliding glass doors, anyone integrating with home automation, and aging-in-place clients. We install Lutron, Hunter Douglas PowerView, and Somfy systems and program them on-site.</p>

      <h3>Ripplefold drapery</h3>
      <p>Modern, clean panels with a uniform wave pattern, hung from a concealed ceiling track. Best for contemporary homes, high-rise condos, and minimalist interiors. Tracks are often paired with motorization for a clean overhead look.</p>

      <h3>Custom valances, cornices, and top treatments</h3>
      <p>Upholstered cornices, soft pleated valances, swag-and-jabot treatments, or board-mounted cornices. These finish the top of the window and can dramatically change the room's formality. Paired with drapery or installed alone.</p>

      <h3>Café and tier curtains</h3>
      <p>Short, often hung mid-window or as a top-half tier. Common in kitchens, bathrooms, and breakfast nooks. Works well in Old Northeast bungalows and Craftsman homes where the window architecture deserves to stay visible.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

